1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,780
 - Hello, and welcome to our chapter on databases.

2
00:00:03,780 --> 00:00:05,560
 We're going to learn a lot in this chapter,

3
00:00:05,560 --> 00:00:08,440
 learn a whole new programming language, SQL,

4
00:00:08,440 --> 00:00:10,620
 and learn how to use that.

5
00:00:10,620 --> 00:00:12,340
 So you're going to need a new piece of software

6
00:00:12,340 --> 00:00:15,520
 to run all of the exercises that I'm going to do

7
00:00:15,520 --> 00:00:17,240
 called SQLite Browser.

8
00:00:17,240 --> 00:00:19,320
 We're using a database called SQLite.

9
00:00:19,320 --> 00:00:20,580
 Go ahead and download this.

10
00:00:20,580 --> 00:00:22,920
 You might have to pause and come back if you like.

11
00:00:22,920 --> 00:00:26,860
 Go to SQLiteBrowser.org and download it and install it.

12
00:00:26,860 --> 00:00:28,780
 While you're doing that,

13
00:00:28,780 --> 00:00:30,420
 let's talk a little bit about the history.

14
00:00:30,420 --> 00:00:34,200
 So, in the old days, 1960s, 1970s,

15
00:00:34,200 --> 00:00:37,200
 I started doing computing in 1975,

16
00:00:37,200 --> 00:00:41,020
 we didn't have a lot of storage.

17
00:00:41,020 --> 00:00:43,740
 I mean, this is 16 gigabytes right here,

18
00:00:43,740 --> 00:00:46,400
 and we didn't even have megabytes.

19
00:00:46,400 --> 00:00:49,580
 I mean, the computer I had had a few megabytes of stuff.

20
00:00:49,580 --> 00:00:52,620
 So, we didn't have a lot of disk drives,

21
00:00:52,620 --> 00:00:56,100
 and so permanent storage was often sequential.

22
00:00:56,100 --> 00:00:58,880
 These tapes, these tape drives that we had,

23
00:00:58,880 --> 00:01:01,760
 tapes and tape drives were the scalable part of storage,

24
00:01:01,760 --> 00:01:03,060
 because you could just make more tapes

25
00:01:03,060 --> 00:01:05,800
 and you could rack 'em up, and so that was our way

26
00:01:05,800 --> 00:01:07,780
 of greatly increasing the storage of the computer.

27
00:01:07,780 --> 00:01:10,700
 The problem they had was, is they were sequential.

28
00:01:10,700 --> 00:01:12,800
 You read it, it advances, read it, it advances,

29
00:01:12,800 --> 00:01:13,640
 read it, it advances.

30
00:01:13,640 --> 00:01:16,740
 Now, interestingly, we've been writing programs that do this,

31
00:01:16,740 --> 00:01:18,020
 that everything we've written so far

32
00:01:18,020 --> 00:01:19,820
 pretty much reads the whole file,

33
00:01:19,820 --> 00:01:22,340
 reads the whole webpage, reads this, everything we read it.

34
00:01:22,340 --> 00:01:24,660
 We read either a loop or read the whole thing,

35
00:01:24,660 --> 00:01:26,200
 and that's because we have plenty of memory,

36
00:01:26,200 --> 00:01:29,160
 but we're still reading sequentially,

37
00:01:29,160 --> 00:01:32,000
 and so the way you would do this

38
00:01:32,000 --> 00:01:34,700
 when you didn't have enough spinning storage

39
00:01:34,700 --> 00:01:37,040
 or online storage is you'd use offline storage,

40
00:01:37,040 --> 00:01:39,100
 but the trick would be that you would sort it.

41
00:01:39,100 --> 00:01:40,460
 So, let's imagine that you're a bank

42
00:01:40,460 --> 00:01:42,320
 and you have a bunch of accounts,

43
00:01:42,320 --> 00:01:44,620
 only a few of which are active on any day,

44
00:01:44,620 --> 00:01:47,140
 and you have a tape that has,

45
00:01:47,140 --> 00:01:49,640
 in account number order from low to high,

46
00:01:49,640 --> 00:01:52,500
 the prior balance, last night's balance

47
00:01:52,500 --> 00:01:54,420
 of every one of your bank accounts,

48
00:01:54,420 --> 00:01:56,660
 and then you do all the transactions

49
00:01:56,660 --> 00:01:59,820
 and you record how much money was taken in or out

50
00:01:59,820 --> 00:02:00,780
 for each account number,

51
00:02:00,780 --> 00:02:02,320
 and then you sort those transactions,

52
00:02:02,320 --> 00:02:04,040
 and then what you do is what we call

53
00:02:04,040 --> 00:02:05,720
 the sequential master update,

54
00:02:05,720 --> 00:02:08,660
 and that is you would write a program

55
00:02:08,660 --> 00:02:11,140
 that would read the first transaction and hold onto it.

56
00:02:11,140 --> 00:02:13,840
 Say, okay, this is count 45.

57
00:02:13,840 --> 00:02:15,740
 Then it would read the first count, like one,

58
00:02:15,740 --> 00:02:17,740
 and it would copy one, and then it'd read two,

59
00:02:17,740 --> 00:02:21,280
 and read like seven, eight, 42, 43,

60
00:02:21,280 --> 00:02:23,160
 then it would read like 44,

61
00:02:23,160 --> 00:02:26,300
 and it would read 45, but now it would change that

62
00:02:26,300 --> 00:02:29,140
 and write the new 45, and read the next thing,

63
00:02:29,140 --> 00:02:30,700
 and so this might be 60,

64
00:02:30,700 --> 00:02:31,980
 and it would read a bunch of stuff

65
00:02:31,980 --> 00:02:33,660
 and copy a bunch of stuff,

66
00:02:33,660 --> 00:02:34,920
 and then it would finally get to 60,

67
00:02:34,920 --> 00:02:37,140
 and it would merge the add, the subtract,

68
00:02:37,140 --> 00:02:39,620
 and so the old balance ended up here,

69
00:02:39,620 --> 00:02:40,800
 and the new balance did here,

70
00:02:40,800 --> 00:02:43,340
 and you had to only make one pass through the data,

71
00:02:43,340 --> 00:02:45,300
 so it was super efficient.

72
00:02:45,300 --> 00:02:47,620
 So, we had all these mechanisms to sort.

73
00:02:47,620 --> 00:02:49,940
 We used to do punch cards and have sorters

74
00:02:49,940 --> 00:02:50,780
 and all these things,

75
00:02:50,780 --> 00:02:54,820
 and then these things would run for hours,

76
00:02:54,820 --> 00:02:56,420
 and if you watch old TV shows,

77
00:02:56,420 --> 00:02:57,680
 these tapes are spinning,

78
00:02:57,680 --> 00:02:59,460
 and these things are running back and forth,

79
00:02:59,460 --> 00:03:02,480
 these are simply reading and writing tapes,

80
00:03:02,480 --> 00:03:04,780
 and that's how we did a lot of data processing,

81
00:03:04,780 --> 00:03:08,500
 because we could store far more on a tape drive

82
00:03:08,500 --> 00:03:09,780
 than we could on a disk,

83
00:03:09,780 --> 00:03:12,220
 and with racks of tape drives,

84
00:03:12,220 --> 00:03:16,020
 we could scale the storage that our computers had,

85
00:03:16,020 --> 00:03:17,580
 and so that's the way we did data processing,

86
00:03:17,580 --> 00:03:20,260
 but it meant that the only way you knew

87
00:03:20,260 --> 00:03:21,540
 what the old balance was,

88
00:03:21,540 --> 00:03:23,620
 was it was the balance as of this morning,

89
00:03:23,620 --> 00:03:24,800
 before your bank started,

90
00:03:24,800 --> 00:03:26,540
 you don't know what the balance was for the day,

91
00:03:26,540 --> 00:03:28,120
 and that led to things like,

92
00:03:28,120 --> 00:03:32,980
 you can never withdraw more than $100 a day,

93
00:03:32,980 --> 00:03:33,820
 or something like that,

94
00:03:33,820 --> 00:03:36,020
 because you don't know what the old balance was,

95
00:03:36,020 --> 00:03:38,020
 or you might go withdraw $100

96
00:03:38,020 --> 00:03:39,260
 at a couple of different branches,

97
00:03:39,260 --> 00:03:43,820
 and so they weren't able to look your stuff up right away.

98
00:03:43,820 --> 00:03:46,660
 Now, it didn't take long

99
00:03:46,660 --> 00:03:48,620
 until the disk drives got better and better and better,

100
00:03:48,620 --> 00:03:52,020
 and you could store the entire accounts,

101
00:03:52,020 --> 00:03:55,020
 all the accounts and their current balances on computers,

102
00:03:55,020 --> 00:03:57,100
 and then the problem becomes,

103
00:03:57,100 --> 00:03:59,620
 is what happens if sort of in the middle of the afternoon,

104
00:03:59,620 --> 00:04:00,780
 you want to update a balance?

105
00:04:00,780 --> 00:04:03,080
 Well, do you want to read all your data,

106
00:04:03,080 --> 00:04:04,460
 and then write a brand new one,

107
00:04:04,460 --> 00:04:07,040
 and let's say that takes like 10 minutes,

108
00:04:07,040 --> 00:04:08,320
 that means for that 10 minutes,

109
00:04:08,320 --> 00:04:11,580
 only one person can be updating their bank balance,

110
00:04:11,580 --> 00:04:15,460
 and so because we could randomly access this data,

111
00:04:15,460 --> 00:04:16,920
 we didn't have to read it all sequentially,

112
00:04:16,920 --> 00:04:20,140
 the trick was, is how do you spread the data out,

113
00:04:20,140 --> 00:04:23,260
 and then how do you make it so you can change a balance,

114
00:04:23,260 --> 00:04:24,820
 this is of course second nature today,

115
00:04:24,820 --> 00:04:26,980
 but how do you make it so you change the balance here

116
00:04:26,980 --> 00:04:28,920
 without changing the balance there,

117
00:04:28,920 --> 00:04:29,820
 and you can have multiple people

118
00:04:29,820 --> 00:04:31,660
 going simultaneously to these things,

119
00:04:31,660 --> 00:04:33,620
 and make sure that you can't say,

120
00:04:33,620 --> 00:04:36,380
 withdraw money at two different locations simultaneously,

121
00:04:36,380 --> 00:04:39,700
 and somehow have your bank balance get corrupted by that.

122
00:04:39,700 --> 00:04:41,460
 So there's a lot of debate on how to do that,

123
00:04:41,460 --> 00:04:45,080
 and in early days, we just did sequential master update,

124
00:04:45,080 --> 00:04:47,540
 but increasingly we wanted to make better use

125
00:04:47,540 --> 00:04:52,020
 of the random nature of our computers, and our storage,

126
00:04:52,020 --> 00:04:54,580
 and so that's what led to databases,

127
00:04:54,580 --> 00:04:58,620
 databases are the science of how you make use

128
00:04:58,620 --> 00:05:03,620
 of rotating random access data, permanent data,

129
00:05:03,620 --> 00:05:06,340
 in a way that allows you to read, modify,

130
00:05:06,340 --> 00:05:07,980
 and update that simultaneously

131
00:05:07,980 --> 00:05:09,460
 from many different locations,

132
00:05:09,460 --> 00:05:12,260
 and yet keep the data completely consistent,

133
00:05:12,260 --> 00:05:14,860
 and so this led to a study of a thing

134
00:05:14,860 --> 00:05:16,220
 called relational databases,

135
00:05:16,220 --> 00:05:17,700
 and relational databases are not

136
00:05:17,700 --> 00:05:21,620
 the only databases that happened,

137
00:05:21,620 --> 00:05:23,260
 we had many other kinds of databases,

138
00:05:23,260 --> 00:05:24,260
 and there was a debate,

139
00:05:24,260 --> 00:05:26,380
 and I remember in the 70s and the 80s,

140
00:05:26,380 --> 00:05:28,700
 there was folks that says, oh no, no,

141
00:05:28,700 --> 00:05:30,260
 there you can do index sequential,

142
00:05:30,260 --> 00:05:31,180
 that's the way to do it,

143
00:05:31,180 --> 00:05:33,780
 and relational databases weren't popular,

144
00:05:33,780 --> 00:05:37,620
 weren't all that popular the first time that I saw them,

145
00:05:37,620 --> 00:05:40,580
 I didn't like relational databases,

146
00:05:40,580 --> 00:05:43,480
 but relational databases had an inherent advantage

147
00:05:43,480 --> 00:05:46,340
 because they were based on some really powerful mathematics,

148
00:05:46,340 --> 00:05:48,460
 and the interesting thing is early on,

149
00:05:48,460 --> 00:05:50,900
 the relational databases were slower,

150
00:05:50,900 --> 00:05:52,860
 but eventually they figured out

151
00:05:52,860 --> 00:05:54,820
 how to sort of bring all the cleverness to bear

152
00:05:54,820 --> 00:05:56,500
 to make relational databases fast,

153
00:05:56,500 --> 00:06:00,560
 and so relational databases are a pretty advanced technology,

154
00:06:00,560 --> 00:06:02,440
 and there are companies like Oracle

155
00:06:02,440 --> 00:06:03,860
 that are very, very wealthy,

156
00:06:03,860 --> 00:06:06,540
 and their primary product for many, many years

157
00:06:06,540 --> 00:06:09,220
 was nothing more than a clever database product,

158
00:06:09,220 --> 00:06:10,780
 a clever piece of software

159
00:06:10,780 --> 00:06:12,540
 that was really good at solving this problem,

160
00:06:12,540 --> 00:06:16,020
 and that's how important this problem was to computing.

161
00:06:16,020 --> 00:06:17,180
 If you read about databases,

162
00:06:17,180 --> 00:06:19,300
 you're going to see two sets of terminology.

163
00:06:19,300 --> 00:06:20,380
 One set of terminology

164
00:06:20,380 --> 00:06:24,020
 comes from the mathematical background,

165
00:06:24,020 --> 00:06:26,420
 and has to do with the underlying math,

166
00:06:26,420 --> 00:06:29,600
 things like relations, tuples, and attributes,

167
00:06:29,600 --> 00:06:33,020
 that's kind of like the fancy math version of it,

168
00:06:33,020 --> 00:06:35,380
 and programmers kind of think of them

169
00:06:35,380 --> 00:06:37,960
 as rows and columns inside of a table,

170
00:06:37,960 --> 00:06:40,260
 and so if you look at sort of fancy theory,

171
00:06:40,260 --> 00:06:42,820
 you'll see words that look like this,

172
00:06:42,820 --> 00:06:45,420
 and they're just full of this in the connection.

173
00:06:45,420 --> 00:06:47,580
 Now, all this is important and true,

174
00:06:47,580 --> 00:06:48,740
 and if you really want to get good,

175
00:06:48,740 --> 00:06:50,780
 you sort of begin to understand the nature

176
00:06:50,780 --> 00:06:53,060
 that we model data at connections

177
00:06:53,060 --> 00:06:55,580
 rather than at sort of intersection points,

178
00:06:55,580 --> 00:06:56,820
 rather than just modeling data

179
00:06:56,820 --> 00:06:59,420
 as a flat file the way we do,

180
00:06:59,420 --> 00:07:02,420
 but for now, we're going to, as programmers,

181
00:07:02,420 --> 00:07:03,600
 think of this as just like,

182
00:07:03,600 --> 00:07:06,260
 oh, it's like a super fast spreadsheet.

183
00:07:06,260 --> 00:07:07,840
 The super fast part is the math.

184
00:07:07,840 --> 00:07:10,420
 For us, the rows, columns, and tables are spreadsheets,

185
00:07:10,420 --> 00:07:12,980
 so it thinks of, think in a spreadsheet of sheets,

186
00:07:12,980 --> 00:07:15,960
 sheet, sheet, sheet, and that's like a table,

187
00:07:15,960 --> 00:07:19,660
 a named thing, like tracks, or albums, artists, or genres,

188
00:07:19,660 --> 00:07:20,940
 and then there is rows,

189
00:07:20,940 --> 00:07:23,540
 and each row has a different kind of data,

190
00:07:23,540 --> 00:07:26,340
 and then there's columns, and we sort of specialize

191
00:07:26,340 --> 00:07:29,380
 the first column in many spreadsheets to say what's in there.

192
00:07:29,380 --> 00:07:30,580
 This is not really the data.

193
00:07:30,580 --> 00:07:32,660
 This is like metadata.

194
00:07:32,660 --> 00:07:34,380
 It's like the title's in this first column.

195
00:07:34,380 --> 00:07:36,700
 That's not really the data, and the data starts here,

196
00:07:36,700 --> 00:07:38,020
 and we have different kinds of data,

197
00:07:38,020 --> 00:07:40,540
 like strings and numbers, et cetera, et cetera,

198
00:07:40,540 --> 00:07:42,820
 for each of the rows.

199
00:07:42,820 --> 00:07:46,620
 And literally, you can get away with this

200
00:07:46,620 --> 00:07:48,420
 as sort of about 80% of databases

201
00:07:48,420 --> 00:07:50,180
 as just a really super cool spreadsheet,

202
00:07:50,180 --> 00:07:54,220
 but under the covers, it is far more powerful than that.

203
00:07:54,220 --> 00:07:59,580
 So one of the early arguments that happened was, again,

204
00:07:59,580 --> 00:08:01,220
 what the programming model for this was,

205
00:08:01,220 --> 00:08:03,940
 and a lot of folks wanted a programming model

206
00:08:03,940 --> 00:08:07,180
 that reflected how the data was actually stored.

207
00:08:07,180 --> 00:08:10,300
 The notion of structured query language came about

208
00:08:10,300 --> 00:08:13,900
 in a way to express what you want it to happen

209
00:08:13,900 --> 00:08:17,060
 and allow that to be sort of a very abstract expression.

210
00:08:17,060 --> 00:08:19,780
 Select all records that meet this criteria,

211
00:08:19,780 --> 00:08:22,740
 not read, read, read, read, read, read.

212
00:08:22,740 --> 00:08:24,340
 And so structured query language

213
00:08:24,340 --> 00:08:27,140
 is not a procedural language.

214
00:08:27,140 --> 00:08:29,900
 It is a imperative language

215
00:08:29,900 --> 00:08:31,700
 where you're simply saying what you want,

216
00:08:31,700 --> 00:08:33,380
 and then somebody writes the loop,

217
00:08:33,380 --> 00:08:34,740
 the database actually does the loop,

218
00:08:34,740 --> 00:08:37,500
 but it's a way for you to avoid actually writing the loop.

219
00:08:37,500 --> 00:08:40,380
 Now, that turns out to be the power of databases

220
00:08:40,380 --> 00:08:42,260
 because the cleverness in how to write the loop

221
00:08:42,260 --> 00:08:44,620
 is a way that you would probably never figure out

222
00:08:44,620 --> 00:08:47,580
 how to be most supremely optimal

223
00:08:47,580 --> 00:08:49,300
 when it comes to writing the loop,

224
00:08:49,300 --> 00:08:50,780
 as you'll see toward the end

225
00:08:50,780 --> 00:08:52,420
 of joining many tables together

226
00:08:52,420 --> 00:08:53,540
 and selecting and throwing a array

227
00:08:53,540 --> 00:08:55,260
 and getting down a count or whatever.

228
00:08:55,260 --> 00:08:58,420
 Someone has figured out how to do that really, really well.

229
00:08:58,420 --> 00:09:01,340
 So the idea was, as you would express,

230
00:09:01,340 --> 00:09:02,580
 we're going to create some data,

231
00:09:02,580 --> 00:09:03,740
 we're going to retrieve some data,

232
00:09:03,740 --> 00:09:05,340
 we're going to insert and delete it.

233
00:09:05,340 --> 00:09:08,780
 Create, read, crud, C-R-U-D.

234
00:09:08,780 --> 00:09:13,140
 Create, read, update, and delete crud.

235
00:09:13,140 --> 00:09:14,500
 And so that's what this does.

236
00:09:14,500 --> 00:09:17,180
 It's a language that does this very simply.

237
00:09:17,180 --> 00:09:21,380
 Now, the applications that we're going to use this for

238
00:09:21,380 --> 00:09:23,460
 are more of a data analysis application.

239
00:09:23,460 --> 00:09:26,500
 We've been doing data analysis through the whole course.

240
00:09:26,500 --> 00:09:28,140
 And the kinds of things that we'll see

241
00:09:28,140 --> 00:09:31,320
 in the remaining chapters is we'll take some raw data file.

242
00:09:31,320 --> 00:09:33,340
 These might actually come across the network.

243
00:09:33,340 --> 00:09:35,140
 And we'll write some Python programs

244
00:09:35,140 --> 00:09:37,780
 to play with that data, parse it,

245
00:09:37,780 --> 00:09:40,660
 clean it up, make sense of it, you know?

246
00:09:40,660 --> 00:09:42,500
 And then write it into a database.

247
00:09:42,500 --> 00:09:43,920
 And this might be a slow process

248
00:09:43,920 --> 00:09:45,120
 or this might be really nasty,

249
00:09:45,120 --> 00:09:47,540
 and this might be a way to have very clean data.

250
00:09:47,540 --> 00:09:49,380
 And then we'll write another Python program

251
00:09:49,380 --> 00:09:51,660
 to sort of read this, read through it,

252
00:09:51,660 --> 00:09:53,580
 and it's all efficient and pretty.

253
00:09:53,580 --> 00:09:55,500
 And then we can produce files

254
00:09:55,500 --> 00:09:56,640
 and maybe we'll visualize it

255
00:09:56,640 --> 00:09:58,940
 or do further analysis in R, Excel,

256
00:09:58,940 --> 00:10:02,160
 or a JavaScript visualization framework.

257
00:10:02,160 --> 00:10:06,280
 And so, in this situation, you will be the person

258
00:10:06,280 --> 00:10:09,100
 who is both sort of writing the programs,

259
00:10:09,100 --> 00:10:12,100
 database administrator, and you can,

260
00:10:12,100 --> 00:10:14,120
 using SQLite Browser, play and look at the database

261
00:10:14,120 --> 00:10:15,400
 kind of in a raw way.

262
00:10:15,400 --> 00:10:16,380
 And the first part of this,

263
00:10:16,380 --> 00:10:18,940
 we are mostly going to be using SQLite Browser

264
00:10:18,940 --> 00:10:20,500
 just to talk straight to a database.

265
00:10:20,500 --> 00:10:23,000
 Later, we'll write Python programs

266
00:10:23,000 --> 00:10:26,240
 that read and write data and visualize the data.

267
00:10:26,240 --> 00:10:28,300
 So this is what we're going to do first.

268
00:10:28,300 --> 00:10:30,720
 And then second, we're going to do this part right here.

269
00:10:30,720 --> 00:10:33,620
 That's the second thing we're going to do.

270
00:10:33,620 --> 00:10:36,540
 Now, another really common use of applications

271
00:10:36,540 --> 00:10:38,420
 and something that if you continue

272
00:10:38,420 --> 00:10:39,940
 learning more about programming,

273
00:10:39,940 --> 00:10:44,500
 is that you will want to write an online application

274
00:10:44,500 --> 00:10:47,580
 like Amazon or a company or Twitter

275
00:10:47,580 --> 00:10:52,700
 that's got a website and it stores dynamic data and databases.

276
00:10:52,700 --> 00:10:55,800
 And so the picture for that is similar but different

277
00:10:55,800 --> 00:10:57,340
 than the picture we're going to start out with.

278
00:10:57,340 --> 00:10:58,880
 And so the way this usually works

279
00:10:58,880 --> 00:11:02,360
 is that you, the end user, uses a web browser,

280
00:11:02,360 --> 00:11:04,100
 talks to the application,

281
00:11:04,100 --> 00:11:07,580
 and the developer writes the application software.

282
00:11:07,580 --> 00:11:11,140
 And that application software stores its data in a database.

283
00:11:11,140 --> 00:11:13,140
 And inside that database,

284
00:11:13,140 --> 00:11:15,460
 we talk to the database using SQL,

285
00:11:15,460 --> 00:11:17,100
 and all the data is actually stored here.

286
00:11:17,100 --> 00:11:18,860
 And the magic happens, the data server

287
00:11:18,860 --> 00:11:22,200
 is that database software that's so precious and valuable.

288
00:11:22,200 --> 00:11:23,500
 And then there's another person,

289
00:11:23,500 --> 00:11:25,420
 often called the database administrator,

290
00:11:25,420 --> 00:11:28,380
 who has access to the direct access to the data.

291
00:11:28,380 --> 00:11:30,500
 And these roles in medium and large project

292
00:11:30,500 --> 00:11:33,840
 are kept separate, mostly because the...

293
00:11:33,840 --> 00:11:37,020
 Mostly because the...

294
00:11:37,020 --> 00:11:42,820
 The production, while it's running and live,

295
00:11:42,820 --> 00:11:44,460
 the developer leaves the data alone

296
00:11:44,460 --> 00:11:47,220
 and works on, say, the next version of the software.

297
00:11:47,220 --> 00:11:49,540
 And then the developer has a test version

298
00:11:49,540 --> 00:11:53,380
 of the application that they run on their computer,

299
00:11:53,380 --> 00:11:54,780
 where they're doing all that stuff.

300
00:11:54,780 --> 00:11:57,760
 So this database administrator is a role

301
00:11:57,760 --> 00:12:01,100
 in a large project where we have to run production

302
00:12:01,100 --> 00:12:05,660
 and keep production careful, keep production in good shape.

303
00:12:05,660 --> 00:12:08,140
 So the database administrator has this responsibility

304
00:12:08,140 --> 00:12:10,140
 for the production aspects of the data.

305
00:12:10,140 --> 00:12:11,900
 And you may be working in a situation

306
00:12:11,900 --> 00:12:13,660
 where you're not actually controlling the data.

307
00:12:13,660 --> 00:12:15,660
 The database server's on different computers,

308
00:12:15,660 --> 00:12:17,220
 you have a little special access,

309
00:12:17,220 --> 00:12:20,420
 and you write programs to sort of read the data.

310
00:12:20,420 --> 00:12:22,680
 And so the database administrator is the person

311
00:12:22,680 --> 00:12:27,600
 who is asked by the organization to administer that data.

312
00:12:27,600 --> 00:12:29,780
 The data that we develop, and we'll do this

313
00:12:29,780 --> 00:12:33,080
 in the second part of these lectures,

314
00:12:33,080 --> 00:12:34,340
 conforms to a data model.

315
00:12:34,340 --> 00:12:35,240
 That's the metadata.

316
00:12:35,240 --> 00:12:36,260
 Is this an integer?

317
00:12:36,260 --> 00:12:37,780
 Is this a string?

318
00:12:37,780 --> 00:12:39,100
 How many columns is this?

319
00:12:39,100 --> 00:12:41,860
 And the data model turns out to be very, very important,

320
00:12:41,860 --> 00:12:42,840
 and there's a lot of science

321
00:12:42,840 --> 00:12:44,640
 to building an effective data model

322
00:12:44,640 --> 00:12:46,220
 that leads to really good performance.

323
00:12:46,220 --> 00:12:48,360
 And it's a collaborative activity

324
00:12:48,360 --> 00:12:50,720
 between the application developers

325
00:12:50,720 --> 00:12:53,900
 and the database administrator to make it so it's efficient,

326
00:12:53,900 --> 00:12:56,880
 runs in production, et cetera, et cetera, et cetera.

327
00:12:56,880 --> 00:13:00,840
 There's a lot of products out there that you may encounter.

328
00:13:00,840 --> 00:13:02,120
 We're going to be using SQLite.

329
00:13:02,120 --> 00:13:04,640
 SQLite's a little tiny database server,

330
00:13:04,640 --> 00:13:06,080
 and it's built into so many things,

331
00:13:06,080 --> 00:13:07,020
 and that's why we like it.

332
00:13:07,020 --> 00:13:09,460
 But if you're going to work at a large organization,

333
00:13:09,460 --> 00:13:10,740
 you could easily run into Oracle,

334
00:13:10,740 --> 00:13:14,420
 which is the number one commercial product.

335
00:13:14,420 --> 00:13:16,420
 Microsoft has a thing called SQL Server,

336
00:13:16,420 --> 00:13:18,180
 which is a commercial product,

337
00:13:18,180 --> 00:13:21,160
 and it's also very popular and very effective.

338
00:13:21,160 --> 00:13:23,460
 The more popular open source,

339
00:13:23,460 --> 00:13:26,380
 there's things called Postgres, there's MySQL,

340
00:13:26,380 --> 00:13:29,560
 and MySQL recently was sort of bought by Oracle,

341
00:13:29,560 --> 00:13:33,520
 and there is a copy of that called MariaDB

342
00:13:33,520 --> 00:13:36,920
 that doesn't belong to Oracle, MariaDB.

343
00:13:36,920 --> 00:13:40,640
 And so, most of the SQL that we're going to learn

344
00:13:40,640 --> 00:13:43,900
 is common across these database systems

345
00:13:43,900 --> 00:13:46,440
 because SQL is a standard,

346
00:13:46,440 --> 00:13:47,700
 but then there are parts that weren't part

347
00:13:47,700 --> 00:13:49,980
 of the original standard where each database vendor

348
00:13:49,980 --> 00:13:51,500
 has done things a little bit different,

349
00:13:51,500 --> 00:13:54,340
 but there is a core common subset

350
00:13:54,340 --> 00:13:56,460
 that does the basic create, read, update,

351
00:13:56,460 --> 00:13:58,380
 and delete operations.

352
00:13:58,380 --> 00:14:00,780
 So SQLite is very popular.

353
00:14:00,780 --> 00:14:04,060
 You probably have it in your cell phone 10, 12 times.

354
00:14:04,060 --> 00:14:06,380
 Your web browser has a database engine in it.

355
00:14:06,380 --> 00:14:09,100
 Your car has a few databases in it.

356
00:14:09,100 --> 00:14:13,460
 And so, SQLite is what's called an embedded database system.

357
00:14:13,460 --> 00:14:15,460
 Python comes built in with it.

358
00:14:15,460 --> 00:14:18,980
 You just import SQLite 3 and away you go.

359
00:14:18,980 --> 00:14:22,580
 And so, it's very, very popular because it's free,

360
00:14:22,580 --> 00:14:25,760
 it's open source, and it's such a tiny little piece

361
00:14:25,760 --> 00:14:27,860
 of software that you just include it

362
00:14:27,860 --> 00:14:29,960
 in other pieces of software and use it

363
00:14:29,960 --> 00:14:31,580
 to solve the data management problems

364
00:14:31,580 --> 00:14:32,760
 of those pieces of software.

365
00:14:32,760 --> 00:14:36,120
 Like your browser might use SQLite to store your bookmarks.

366
00:14:36,120 --> 00:14:37,660
 Now you think, oh, there's only how many bookmarks

367
00:14:37,660 --> 00:14:40,720
 can you have, but what if there you need it to be fast?

368
00:14:40,720 --> 00:14:42,980
 And what if there's people that have 10,000 bookmarks?

369
00:14:42,980 --> 00:14:44,340
 There probably are.

370
00:14:44,340 --> 00:14:45,340
 Do you still want it fast?

371
00:14:45,340 --> 00:14:46,340
 Do you want to be able to search?

372
00:14:46,340 --> 00:14:50,820
 And so, you get all that by using a database like SQLite.

373
00:14:50,820 --> 00:14:52,700
 And so, again, we're going to encourage you

374
00:14:52,700 --> 00:14:55,920
 to download the SQLite browser so you can follow along

375
00:14:55,920 --> 00:14:58,660
 with what we're going to do coming up next.

376
00:14:58,660 --> 00:15:00,460
 And so, here is the SQLite browser.

377
00:15:00,460 --> 00:15:01,860
 Here's what it looks like.

378
00:15:01,860 --> 00:15:03,780
 And it's just a desktop application.

379
00:15:03,780 --> 00:15:05,740
 And coming up next, we'll start playing

380
00:15:05,740 --> 00:15:09,900
 with this desktop application and see how it works.

